            "least": 63,
            "why": "A reader asks what you called and what you passed it. Both names sat in the `variable` family and read as one colour in 40 of 65 themes — in gruvbox-dark-medium both at #83a598 — so a call spent three to five colours inside and none of them on the two words that answer. Canon splits by language SURFACE rather than by call against definition: html, Vue, JSX, Liquid, Handlebars and MediaWiki's own `{{T|name=v}}` all take entity.other.attribute-name for a named argument on a tag or template surface. Under the argument's own family the pair read 25 of 65; under the surface's, 57. RE-SEATED 50 to 45 on 2026-09-17, on the ruling that a called thing reads as a widget does: a macro name carries entity.name.function.macro and a widget name entity.name.function.widget, themes rule on the entity.name.function root the two share, so a reader meets one gesture across call and widget, and the pair then parts on the argument's surface family alone at 45 of 65. The control settles whose loss this reads as — a widget against the attribute it carries clears its own floor unmoved under the same ruling, so the five themes track the SHARED NAME rather than any weakening on the argument's side. The argument key then took a second name on 2026-09-18, `support.type.property-name.argument` appended after the published attribute name, which stays first for every theme ruling on it: a theme painting `entity.name.function` and `entity.other.attribute-name` alike — gruvbox-dark-medium paints both #fabd2f — rules on the property-name family apart, and the pair measured 63 of 65. RE-SEATED 45 to 63 on that measurement. The call's name keeps its widget family, and the widget's attribute keeps its own, so the widget-against-call floor stands where the one-namespace ruling seated it.",
